THIRD-PARTY DEPENDENCIES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Zuken Shield does not vendor, bundle, or redistribute any third-party source
code or libraries. Every dependency below is installed separately — from the
distribution's package archive, or from PyPI — and remains under its own
licence. The Debian package declares them as dependencies; APT resolves them.

  PySide6 (Qt for Python)  LGPL-3.0-only OR GPL-2.0-only OR GPL-3.0-only
                           Shield relies on the LGPL-3.0 option.
                           Modules used: QtCore, QtGui, QtWidgets.
  Qt 6 libraries           LGPL-3.0 or GPL-2.0 (Shield relies on LGPL-3.0)
  pyqtgraph                MIT
  scapy                    GPL-2.0, with BSD-2 and BSD-3 components
  manuf                    Apache-2.0 or GPL-3.0 (dual)
  reportlab                BSD-3-Clause
  llama-cpp-python         MIT      (optional; no feature requires it)
  diskcache                Apache-2.0 (pulled in by llama-cpp-python)

External command-line tools invoked at runtime, never redistributed:
arp-scan, nmap, tcpdump, nftables, bpftrace, journalctl, notify-send, pkexec.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
LGPL-3.0 OBLIGATIONS (PySide6 AND Qt 6)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Shield uses PySide6 and Qt 6 under the LGPL-3.0 option. Because Shield links
against them dynamically and does not ship them, the obligations are met as
follows:

  * Notice — this file states that Shield uses PySide6 and Qt 6 under
    LGPL-3.0, and identifies the modules used.
  * Replaceability — Shield bundles no Qt or PySide6 libraries. They are
    installed as separate system packages, and a user may upgrade, patch, or
    replace them through the package manager without touching Shield. No
    static linking and no private copies are used.
  * Source availability — Shield does not redistribute these libraries, so the
    obligation to supply their source rests with whoever does. Their source is
    available from the distribution and from the Qt Project.
  * No additional restrictions — Shield's Apache-2.0 licence places no
    restriction on modifying, replacing, or reverse-engineering the LGPL
    libraries for debugging your own modifications.

No GPL-only Qt module is used. Shield imports only QtCore, QtGui, and
QtWidgets, all of which are offered under LGPL-3.0.

An earlier version of Shield used PyQt6, which Riverbank Computing offers only
under GPL-3.0 or a commercial licence. That dependency was removed in favour of
PySide6 precisely so the project's own source can remain Apache-2.0.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
PACKET CAPTURE IS A SEPARATE COMPONENT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

scapy is distributed under the GPL-2.0. The Shield core does not import it.

Packet capture lives in a separate program, package, and process:

  shield-monitor             Apache-2.0 core. Depends on no GPL-2.0 library.
                             Does not import scapy — enforced by an AST scan
                             over the whole core in the test suite.

  shield-packet-collector    Optional component. Depends on python3-scapy and
                             declares that dependency itself. Ships its own
                             .deb, its own pyproject, and its own systemd unit.

The two exchange newline-delimited JSON over a local Unix socket. The message
schema is closed: nine observation types, a fixed key set, regex-checked
addresses, bounded numbers and strings, and a sanity window on timestamps. No
pickle, no Python objects, no command channel. The core treats the helper as
untrusted input, because the helper runs as root and reads packets off the
network.

This separation is architectural. It makes the licence boundary explicit and
testable. It is not a legal conclusion, and none is offered here — whether a
particular way of distributing or combining these components has a particular
legal consequence is a question for the maintainers and, if they wish, their
own counsel.

Shield works without the helper. Endpoint telemetry, file integrity, journal
and auditd ingestion, device discovery, correlation, the evidence graph,
incident reports, guided Q&A, and the response workflow are unaffected.
Installing it adds ARP, DHCP, ICMP and NDP observation; TCP handshake
visibility used by port-scan detection; outbound DNS observation; and the live
bytes-per-second graph.

The helper's own licence position must be reviewed separately by whoever
distributes it; it is not covered by the Apache-2.0 licence of the core.
